# cad/scripts/runtime-bundle.manifest — the census of the BUILT runtime bundle
#
# One `name version` per line: every distribution that has a .dist-info in the
# built bundle's site-packages, canonical (PEP 503) name, lower-cased, "-" for
# "_". This file is the licence gate's input BECAUSE the gate runs in CI on a
# runner that never builds a bundle: the inventory in notice_inventory.py is
# checked against this committed census, and the census is checked against a
# real bundle by `gen_notice.py --verify-bundle <site-packages>` on the build
# legs, which is where drift surfaces.
#
# WHY NOT `pip list` INSIDE THE STAGE INTERPRETER. The staged interpreter's
# sys.path picks up the developer's ~/.local/lib/python3.12/site-packages, so
# `pip list` reports distributions that are not in the bundle at all and
# reports the USER's version where both are installed. The census is produced
# by reading *.dist-info/METADATA out of one directory instead:
#
#     python3 cad/scripts/gen_notice.py --census <stage>/lib/python3.12/site-packages
#
# Only build123d and python-fcl are pinned in runtime-bundle.lock; everything
# else here is transitive and unconstrained, so an upstream release changes
# these versions. That is the point — a version change here is a licence text
# that has to be re-checked, and --verify-bundle is what makes someone look.
#
# python-fcl is in the lock (PIP_NO_DEPS_PKGS) and is listed below, but it was
# NOT present in the stage this census was taken from — that stage predates the
# pin. --verify-bundle on a build leg is what turns that claim into a fact.

